{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788311251,"nonce":"44S8xFWh0GvsTFxfRvAd0/5FTt0=","title":"**Filed mid-run (m1-20260901), Evan's call. Loose — needs verification against the bench before anyone builds it.** Evan, watching the window: *\"you also set to mine a tiny bit of marble and limestone? was that intentional? there's a 'mine vein' button.\"* It was not intentional, and the run has been told to carry on mining the extra rock for now.","message":"Aiming at the compacted-steel face 20 cells east of the base, the run issued:\n\n```\ndesignate {type:\"mine\", rect:[131,116,6,10], max_cells:600}   -\u003e accepted 14 of 60\n```\n\nThe intent was ~11,000 steel out of the 274 `MineableSteel` cells the map carries. What was actually designated is 14 cells of *whatever rock was exposed on that rect*, which on this face is mostly sandstone and marble. The colony will spend Mining work producing stone chunks it did not ask for.\n\nNothing refused, nothing warned, and the result envelope was truthful: `accepted: 14, targeted: 60`. There is no field in it that distinguishes \"14 cells of ore\" from \"14 cells of worthless rock\".\n\n\n\nThe view that was used to pick the rect reports:\n\n```\n\"%\": \"sandstone | marble | compacted steel\"\n```\n\nOne glyph for the thing worth mining and the two things that are not. A rect chosen off that view cannot be aimed. This is not a defect in the alphabet — collision is the documented cost of a fixed-width channel, and `map-view` says so — but it means **`map-view` is the wrong instrument for siting a mine designation**, and nothing says so anywhere.\n\nThe honest read exists and the run already had it in hand: `nearest {def:\"MineableSteel\", from:\"114,129\"}` returns exact cells — (133,122), (133,121), (134,120), (134,119) — with `pool: 274`. It was used to *find* the face and then not used to *designate* it.\n\n\n`DesignationVerbs.cs:72` registers it:\n\n```\nAdd(\"mine-vein\", () =\u003e new Designator_MineVein(), DesignationDefOf.MineVein,\n    \"RimWorld/Designator_MineVein.CanDesignateCell\");\n```\n\nSo the verb exists. What does not exist is any route by which an agent following the playbook would reach for it. **`checklists/turn.md` §materials-designation-loop names `mine` and only `mine`:**\n\n\u003e act: `designate {type:\"chop\", rect:…}` / `designate {type:\"mine\", rect:…}` over the next batch — one rect, one call.\n\nThe checklist told the run to do the wrong thing for ore, in the moment the check fired. Evan's reading is that this \"probably needs to be hooked up\" — whether that means the verb is registered but non-functional, or merely undocumented, **is the first thing to verify**: the run did not test `mine-vein` before filing this.\n\nWorth noting while checking: `DesignationVerbs.cs:217` already documents an interaction between the two — `Designator_Mine.CanDesignateThing` rejects a cell that already carries a `MineVein` designation — so the two are not independent and the order of designation matters.\n\n\nStone chunks feed `TableStonecutter` (unlocked at start on this fixture) and 1,824 chunks are already lying on the map, so mining *more* rock for blocks is close to pure waste here. On a mountain base it would be the point. The verb cannot know which, so the answer is a report, not a refusal: **say what was designated.**\n\n\n1. `designate {type:\"mine\"|\"mine-vein\"}` reports the composition of what it accepted — a per-def rollup of the designated cells (`MineableSteel: 4, MineableSandstone: 8, MineableMarble: 2`) and, where the game publishes it, the `mineableThing` / `mineableYield` those cells will actually produce. A rect aimed at ore and landing on rock is then visible in the envelope that accepted it.\n2. `mine-vein` is exercised on a bench: designate one `MineableSteel` cell, assert the designation propagates along the vein the way `Designator_MineVein.CanDesignateCell` intends, and assert the interaction at `DesignationVerbs.cs:217` (a `Mine` designation refused on a cell already carrying `MineVein`) is reported rather than silently dropped.\n3. `checklists/turn.md` §materials-designation-loop is amended: ore is `mine-vein` seeded off `nearest {def:\"Mineable\u003cX\u003e\"}`, plain rock for blocks is `mine` over a rect, and **`map-view` is named as the wrong instrument for either** because its `%` glyph collapses ore into rock. The `becomes:`/`retire-when:` lines are re-derived at the same time.\n4. A playbook lesson or a template annotation carries the general form: a collapsed-alphabet channel is for topology, a def-keyed query is for identity, and a designation is an identity question. `2.5`'s own independence argument (`channel.alphabet`, never compare a glyph from one channel against a glyph from another) is the precedent to cite.\n5. `accept/\u003cid\u003e-mine-vein.py` covers 1 and 2 on a bench.\n\n\nThat the 14 designations already placed are harmful — they are not, on this map, beyond the Mining hours. The run keeps them (Evan: *\"it's fine to mine extra stuff for now\"*) and will seed the steel separately once this is settled.","files":null}]}